Z||000000||ka4nkf||01-18-2001||10:47 AM||||Orge<BR>if dielectric grease is non-conductive, then it looks to me that if you put it on a terminal connection then you would not have a solid connection. maybe you should put the connector together then put the grease around it. am i right or wrong?||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||01-18-2001||12:33 PM||||Wrong...put it on the connection. I use it on alot of things in the car (Battery term, ignition conrole mod, ect)...BTW, its O<B>gr</B>e...NOT O<B>rg</B>e....as in "oh-grrr"..not "or-gee"..hes no sex party lol.....well, then again, I dont know him...ehhh......lol  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||RNGCRSH||01-18-2001||06:41 PM||||If it were non-conductive it would be called non-conductive grease. Instead its called dialectric grease. a dialectric is a substance in which its conductance changes with thicknes. when put on electrical conections it is to thin to make a difference in conductance. when placed on a terminal conection it is actualy more conductive than nothing at all. it will also reduce corossion at the connection.<P>I use silicone grease at work for 1/2 Million Volt connections and have no problems, if I did not use it I would be in big trouble.<p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 01-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-18-2001||08:54 PM||||Your wrong!||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-18-2001||11:08 PM||||The stuff is a mix of several silicon compounds. I know it doesn't make sense but it works the way it works. As you assemble the connection the "grease" is displaced yet it still fills and seals any gaps to keep out air, water, etc. remember, while they may look like it. none of the surfaces involved is truely smooth.<P>Think of it kind of like an IDC crimp for a computer cable. As you squash the crimp it punches thru the insulation on the wire. Same thing happens with the grease.<P>The big mistake many people make is to use too much of the stuff in allot of applications. Spark plug wires are a good example. Many people will stuff a big gob in the boots. Problem is this can trap air in the boot. The air gets hot, expands, and pushes the wires off the plugs. (Magnecor gets enough mail about this item it's covered in their FAQ)<P>All you need is a little bit smeared on the terminals. A little of this stuff goes a long way.<P>Another thing I do is work it into the strands of a wire before I crimp the ends. This prevents crud wicking up the wire. I'll also pack some into certain types of crimps.<P><BR>(My7.... nope, none of those kinds of parties here.... that's a troll thing.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||RNGCRSH||01-19-2001||07:48 PM||||Who is wrong?<P>If I am then I state Webster’s definition of dielectric strength:<P>The maximum electric field intensity that a given material can sustain without loosing its non-conductive properties.<P>Silicon grease is a better dielectric (about 5 times) than air its dielectric strength is high enough that it will seem to be a non-conductor on most electrical connections. <P>In the case of spark plugs boot, I agree with Ogre about using too much. But the reason you use it to promote better conductivity at the terminal. (what you said above about not smooth surfaces applies. irregularities in the surface will couse acing. the grease stops micro discharge at thes points thus increasing conductivity)If it were not used you run the risk of arcing in the boot, which would result in a cooler spark in the chamber, thus a less efficient burn<P>I have 15 years experience in the field of high KV (up to 1/2 million Volt) electronics plus an electrical engineering degree. I fully understand how electronics work at the molecular level.<P>I am not tying to start any kind of flame war but when you say someone is wrong please back it up, with proof!<P>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 01-19-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 01-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-20-2001||12:32 AM||||RNGCRSH, I can't say much at the voltages you are dealing with..... in auto applications is it used simply to prevent corrosion and electolysis in the connection. <P>The hottest OE ignition is probalby waste spark DIS and even that is only dealing with 40kv max. Is that enough to operate in the modes you are describing???? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||RNGCRSH||01-20-2001||02:41 AM||||I modified my previous post to be more clear and to revise; I did mistype the dielectric strength of Silicon grease in relation to air. (Been up to long when I made the reply) when used in a low voltage circuit you are absolutely correct, corrosion prevention only. At higher voltages beginning at about 1.5KV and up you begin too run the risk of discharging to another conductor. In the case of an ignition system you want the spark to jump the gap at the plug, if there is any air gap that is smaller than the gap in the plug it will jump there instead. The grease prevents that and therefor promotes better conductivity<P>In the case of the work I do. The rules of conventional electrical theory don’t quite apply, as you would think.  When you go above 100KV materials begin to do strange things.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 01-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||01-20-2001||08:21 AM||||I don't think that dialectric grease has to conduct electricity. I think that the automotive electrical connectors are interference-fit things. Whatever you coat them with, they would bite into the mating connector. So I think the only job the grease has to do is keep out water and dirt.||||reg||1
